  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\n- Showers and isolated thunder today\n\nNot much change to the going forecast. Radar shows light showers\nmoving east across Lower MI and extending west to Nebraska. Given\nMUCAPE only around 100 j/k, it's no surprise there isn't much\nlightning around. However, HRRR shows SBCAPE increasing to several\nhundred j/kg by this afternoon and so isolated thunder remains a\npossibility. Current shear values are around 20kts but progd to\nincrease to 40-50 kts this afternoon. Thus, a few showers or\nisolated thunderstorms may be able to produce some gusty winds,\nbut instability remains lacking. Moisture isn't though. PWATs\nremain high...aoa 2 inches and so locally heavy rainfall remains\npossible. Once the cold front moves through after 18z, we'll see\nthe showers move southeast with it. Skies will clear late in the\nday.\n\n- Small chance of weekend showers\n\nHigh pressure will create a couple of dry days tomorrow and Friday\nbefore the next cold front moves through. Not a lot of change\nthere either. It's a weak front with not much of a thermal\ngradient and the accompanying low will be near James Bay. Moisture\nreturn ahead of it is anemic and so PoPs remain low; much of the\nweekend will be dry. Highs will be in the upper 70s to around 80,\nbut dewpoints will remain in the mid to upper 60s creating humid\nconditions. \n\n- Higher chance of showers/storms Monday\n\nA strong short wave moving onshore over the Pacific NW will move\neast this weekend creating a northern Plains low that will drag a\ncold front across Lower MI early next week. this short wave is\nprogd to be fairly potent and so showers and thunderstorms will be\nin the forecast Monday into Tuesday. \n\nModels show a 50kt LLJ Monday afternoon coupled with PWATs aoa 2\ninches and 40-50kts of shear along with SBCAPE around 1200 j/kg.\nThose are decent dynamics for storm development and we'll continue\nto keep an eye on this potential development.",
